1795115974497553	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.move	bn-1uq	{"state":"done"}	blake3:xPHg_mi80MZl0GinApu0n5MtwbRPXB4PfrL15PNWDiQ
1795186102273896	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-34a	{"description":"sippycup's call ledger reserves every call of a command before the first packet and releases, with a reason, each call for which no INVITE can have left (preflight failed, registration failed, step never started). hallpass has spends only: a count never goes down. Today a caller must spend at the moment of first send (hallpass spend --to, one call at a time), which gives 'a count from first send' but no up-front refusal of a whole matrix. Decide: (a) stay spend-only and document it (current state, docs/INTEGRATING.md), or (b) add a release line to the ledger chain (seq of the spend, amount given back, reason; never below zero; shown by hallpass ledger; carried through adoption). (b) is the first thing in hallpass that makes a count smaller, so it is the owner's decision, not a papercut.","kind":"task","size":"m","title":"Release a spend whose traffic never left (a caller that reserves before its first packet)"}	blake3:CAbbwi2sAi4dHoX0ta-3UyEEWwdknNwcjtXBMhlVlKk
1795186102369079	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1na	{"description":"sippycup's ready, rehearse, dry runs and plans read the same rule as the real reservation and print the same text, without counting. hallpass has no such call: status --format json gives counters[].remaining and permits gives the destination and window, but a caller has to combine them itself and the refusal texts differ from spend's. Add spend --dry-run (library: a flag on SpendRequest or a would_spend function) that takes the same locks for reading, decides exactly as spend does (digest, window, destination, counter, ceiling, ledger and witness state) and records nothing; JSON result would-allow/refused. Must fail closed on everything spend fails closed on, including an unusable witness.","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"Ask whether a spend would fit without recording it (dry run with the same decision path)"}	blake3:4RyHPH7qu8rPn0VtNzmEkz_qzPgk0QTxhenE9ufBJNo
1795186102460703	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2qt	{"description":"sippycup's practice benches reuse one approval reference in many directories by design (BENCH_APPROVAL_IDS: LOCAL-PRACTICE-ONLY, LOCAL-PRACTICE-REHEARSAL, ROUTED-REHEARSAL), and practice-target up records a new approval window with a fresh budget on every start. hallpass refuses a second pass file with a known id (ledger-missing, ledger-changed) and never resets a count. No hallpass change is needed if each bench start writes a new pass with its own id (for example practice-20261120T090000Z) for loopback destinations only; then the witness directory grows by one small file per start. Decide whether that is the answer, or whether hallpass needs a marked bench pass (and if so, how it is kept from ever naming a non-loopback destination).","kind":"task","size":"m","title":"Bench passes: one id used in many directories, and a new window and budget on each start"}	blake3:F97bS3qgL2TfSDab6TQz1X75coV69RQ_xGyZ1p96oWM
1795186102551410	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3ql	{"description":"sippycup approval ids are 1-128 printable characters (CHG-1234, but also text with spaces); a hallpass id is 1-64 of letters, digits, . _ -, and is also a file name in the witness directory. sippycup also accepts an approval with validUntil only; hallpass requires valid_from. A caller that writes passes from sippycup scope files needs one documented rule: where the owner's reference goes (approval.reference keeps it verbatim, up to 200 characters), how the id is derived so that the same approval always gets the same id (per-approval counts depend on it), and what valid_from is when the owner gave none (hallpass should keep requiring it: the operator writes the time the approval was given).","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"Map an owner's approval reference to a pass id, and approvals that state no start"}	blake3:aQotOtCbez2DAKAueu6VFHMbEwJsTPlPNELFlWVhvTw
1795186102645925	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1du	{"description":"A hallpass ledger entry holds counter, amount, time, pass digest and a free note of 500 characters. sippycup's reserve entries hold the command, the source file and a reservation id that each run directory points back to, and its cross-check accounts for every run directory. With spend --to the decision now includes the destination, but the entry does not say which. Adding fields is a ledger format change: older hallpass versions fail closed on unknown fields (as they do on adoption lines). Until then a caller can put its own run id in --note and store the returned seq in its run directory. Decide the fields (to, name, label, caller, ref) and the version rule.","kind":"task","size":"m","title":"Record what a spend paid for in its ledger entry (target, caller, run reference)"}	blake3:nz-1DJpWP1o-rOpxMPUEW9YMIBCz7lqbHsgndAUTNQY
1795186102739742	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3z6	{"description":"sippycup's media guard checks every signalling and media address of a call (findings 3 and 17: no RTP or RTCP leaves for an unlisted media address). With the CLI that is one process start per address: permits for each media address, then spend --to for the signalling address. clap refuses a repeated --to today (exit 1), which is the safe behaviour. Add repeated --to on permits and spend (all must be allowed; the refusal names the first one that is not; JSON targets as a list) so one call covers one unit of work. Library callers can already loop over Pass::permits under one load.","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"Several targets in one question (signalling, RTP and RTCP addresses of one call)"}	blake3:Ih1N87zeORfaRQDqn5ysbBQhctOO746wJzs_3eklvlI
1795186102830112	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-12w	{"description":"sippycup limits authorization.networks in total: at most one IPv4 /8 and one IPv6 /64 across all networks. hallpass caps each destination (/24 and /64, or /8 and /32 with whole_network_approved) but not their sum: 1024 destinations of /8 each validate. Decide a total (addresses per family across all destinations, with whole_network_approved counted) and report it in check and status.","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"A total breadth rule across destinations"}	blake3:0YUvXpRj4YcyR9mkETJ2klJ8Y4ChfYoaeg8M3Ouf9WE
1795186102923580	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-13z	{"description":"The window uses the caller's clock. A clock set back reopens an expired window, and nothing notices: spend does not compare now with the time of the last ledger entry (tests/cli.rs even spends at 10:00 and then at 09:00). A plain refusal is not certain: two processes take their clocks before waiting for the lock, so entries are legitimately out of order by the lock wait, and one entry written with a clock far in the future would lock the pass until then. Decide a tolerance and a recovery (for example: refuse when now is more than N seconds before the last entry, with hallpass adopt as the recorded way past it).","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"Notice a clock that went backwards (now is earlier than the last ledger entry)"}	blake3:ortGkHWfPsjiTEj4jn4BeG8i4jUCHze2oI87S9eioAM
1795186103015134	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-649	{"description":"spend takes now from the caller (the CLI reads the clock at start) and then waits for the witness and ledger locks without limit. If another process holds the lock for long (stopped, or a slow file system), the spend is decided for the instant before the wait and can be recorded after valid_until. --duration covers this when the caller's duration includes the wait. Options: try_lock with a bounded wait and a clear error; or let the CLI re-read the clock after the lock (needs a clock callback in the library, which today takes now as a value on purpose).","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"Bound the wait for the ledger lock, or take the clock again after it"}	blake3:Q_YKn0N2jGlsLOnNWorb8XYqv8HeHeqizCsRO5eO1Bc
1795186103105841	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2rf	{"description":"By design (DESIGN.md, 'State directory failures') the read-only commands answer with a warning and witness: not-checked when the state directory or witness is unusable: permits then says allowed, exit 0, with the reason in warnings. spend fails closed in the same situation, and a caller that follows docs/INTEGRATING.md sends only after spend. A caller that uses permits alone (no ceilings) gets an allowed while a state file is unreadable. Either keep it (the destination and window answer does not depend on the count) or make permits exit 1 like spend; tests/cli.rs an_unusable_state_directory_fails_closed_for_spends pins today's behaviour.","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"Decide whether permits should refuse when the witness cannot be checked"}	blake3:fFI-pLsomXmf_mflvnmn-ag9B9m0bvh7JbQDCYAmaTw
1795186103195199	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1my	{"description":"DESIGN.md and PASS-FORMAT.md speak of what 'hallpass 0.1' did and what holds 'since 0.2' (notes, adoption lines, the witness), but Cargo.toml is still 0.1.0, and hallpass --version prints that. An integrating tool cannot tell which behaviour it is calling. Bump the version, and consider a hallpass field in every JSON object (or document that fields are only ever added).","kind":"task","size":"xs","title":"Version drift: the crate says 0.1.0, the docs describe 0.2 behaviour, and JSON output carries no version"}	blake3:0qaas3qp2U3CL7DzLwRxaz1uCFcl2e1r3ScjvMVaPkM
1795186103290317	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-210	{"description":"load refuses a pass that group or others may write, but does not look at who owns it: a pass in a shared directory that belongs to another user and has mode 0644 is read as the operator's own approval, and that user can change it. Comparing the file's uid with the uid of the process needs geteuid (libc, or the owner of /proc/self), which the crate has avoided so far. Decide whether to add the check (and the dependency).","kind":"task","size":"xs","title":"A pass owned by another user is accepted when its mode is 0644"}	blake3:0gCLVXfL50vwrBLZcX2MXCpODJ5_iEBIpxwZHDU0XEk
